/**
\fn Collider& pdg::Part::setupFrameCollider(int mode, int alphaThreshold)
\brief Set up collision geometry from this Part's current artwork.

Reuses .collider when present, preserving its configuration, filters, material,
handlers and body association. Each call replaces the previous geometry source
and explicit shapes. Later Collider add methods create persistent extra shapes.
Queries and contacts refresh source geometry; source shape IDs survive artwork
changes and disappearance/reappearance. See \ref collider_geometry_sources.

Owned Drawing content takes precedence. Bounds uses its current local bounding
rectangle, including element transforms. AlphaMask requires exactly one image
or image-strip element using fit_Fill; setImage() supplies suitable content.
It samples the selected Attributes.frame(), with an invalid frame falling back
to zero as in drawing. Alpha ignores stroke, fill overlays and opacity effects.
Pixel edits to an existing Image require calling setupFrameCollider() again.
Unsupported vector/multi-element/other-fit alpha sources throw; use Bounds for
those Drawings. Replacing content later with an unsupported alpha source also
causes source queries to throw until corrected or another source is selected.

Without owned content, an explicitly bound animation image (from
bindToAnimationBinding()) supplies its current frame and pivot. A bone or socket
binding alone does not select artwork. No artwork produces no source shapes;
logical Part width/height do not invent a frame. Part transforms, including
reflection, apply normally. Artwork changes do not change body mass or inertia.

\param mode frameCollider_AlphaMask (default) or frameCollider_Bounds.
\param alphaThreshold Integer 1 through 255, default 128; alpha at or above this value collides.
\return This Part's Collider. The reference is borrowed from the Part.
\note Owned image/Drawing content and imported image-frame adapters require a
GUI-capable build. Enable layer collisions for contacts; queries work without a
layer or body. Invalid setup preserves the previous collider/source.

\par Example
The enabled pose on hero contains an image binding named hand_art.
\code{.cpp}
auto* grip = hero.createPart("gripArt"); // borrowed from hero
 grip->bindToAnimationBinding("hand_art");
 auto& collider = grip->setupFrameCollider(pdg::frameCollider_AlphaMask);
 collider.setSensor(true);
\endcode
\see setupAnimationCollider(), setDrawing(), setImage(), Collider
*/

/**
\fn Collider& pdg::Part::setupAnimationCollider(const std::string& boxName)
\brief Select one authored animation box as this Part's collision source.

boxName must identify a box in the owning Sprite's enabled animation pose;
image and point bindings are rejected. The Part need not already be bound.
Setup aligns the shape with the authored box, accounting for the Part's local
transform at setup. Playback and the final pose update the box relative to the
Part's current parent frame; subsequent local Part edits carry that shape.
Singular parent frames yield no source shape until invertible again. A singular
local transform at setup throws.

The box name is explicit: unlike Sprite.setupAnimationCollider(), this method
does not collect every box. Inactive boxes have no shape; reappearing boxes keep
their source ID. Explicit Collider additions remain alongside the source.

Replacing/disabling the pose or transferring the Part to another Sprite retires
the old source without silently rebinding it. Select a current source before
saving that Part; snapshots reject stale authored sources. Valid snapshots in
both resource modes retain the selected box, setup offset and source IDs.

\param boxName Required, exact authored box name; empty or unknown names throw.
\return This Part's Collider. The reference is borrowed from the Part.
\note Requires Spriter support and an enabled owner pose, in GUI or headless
builds. No physics body or Chipmunk solver is required for collider queries.
Setup preserves configuration and replaces geometry, like setupFrameCollider().

\par Example
The enabled pose on hero contains a hand bone and a hitbox box.
\code{.cpp}
auto* hit = hero.createPart("handHit");
hit->bindToBone(hero.getAnimationRig()->findBone("hand"));
hit->setupAnimationCollider("hitbox").setSensor(true);
\endcode
\see setupFrameCollider(), Sprite::setupAnimationCollider(), bindToBone()
*/
